# Test classes that cannot run in an image built by the Native Image LLVM backend.
# One fnmatch pattern per line; `#` starts a comment. `_native_unittest` passes this file to
# `--blacklist` only when the build arguments contain `--tool:llvm-backend` and the caller did not
# pass a --blacklist of its own, so a build without the backend runs exactly the same tests as
# before. Every entry names the reason; an entry without one does not belong here.
#
# The Foreign Function and Memory API is switched off with the LLVM backend by upstream policy:
# SubstrateOptions.isForeignAPIEnabled() returns !useLLVMBackend(), and
# SubstrateOptions.ConcealedOptions.validateForeignAPISupport refuses an explicit
# -H:+ForeignAPISupport with "Support for the Foreign Function and Memory API is not available with
# the LLVM backend". Without ForeignFunctionsFeature these tests cannot even link a MethodHandle to
# a downcall stub. Not a Windows issue: linux-amd64 behaves the same.
com.oracle.svm.test.foreign.*

# Continuations, and therefore real (unbound) virtual threads, do not exist with the LLVM backend:
# ContinuationsFeature.afterRegistration (ContinuationsFeature.java:89) computes
#   supported = hostSupport && !DeoptimizationSupport.enabled() && !SubstrateOptions.useLLVMBackend()
#               && SubstrateControlFlowIntegrity.singleton().continuationsSupported()
# and when it is false the image initializes jdk.internal.vm.Continuation at run time so that any
# use throws, while Thread.ofVirtual() hands out ThreadBuilders$BoundVirtualThread. Enabling
# -H:+VMContinuations explicitly is a UserError naming the LLVM backend as the reason.
# The four JFR tests below need what a bound virtual thread cannot give: the first runs its body on
# a virtual thread and gets "NoClassDefFoundError: Could not initialize class
# jdk.internal.vm.Continuation" -> "Event: jdk.ThreadSleep not found in recording!", the others
# wait for jdk.VirtualThreadStart, for an unmounted virtual thread to leave the thread constant
# pool, and for a virtual event thread on an old-object sample. Same four, on both platforms:
# linux-amd64 run 35371119827 (256 tests, 4 failures), windows-amd64 run 35371088929 (229, 4).
# Every other virtual-thread test in the suite passes under the backend.
com.oracle.svm.test.jfr.TestJavaLevelVirtualThreadEvents
com.oracle.svm.test.jfr.TestMirrorEvents
com.oracle.svm.test.jfr.TestVirtualThreadsUnreferencedStreaming
com.oracle.svm.test.jfr.oldobject.TestOldObjectVirtualThreadSampleEvent
